<p class="lead">You are about to launch a new contractor website. You have spent weeks (maybe months) on design, copy, and functionality. But one broken form, one missing meta description, or one slow-loading page can turn that launch from a revenue driver into a credibility killer. This is the website launch checklist we use at OnwardCraft before every contractor site goes live. It covers technical validation, content review, SEO setup, and launch day execution. Follow it step by step, and you will not miss a thing.</p>

<h2>Why Most Contractor Website Launches Underperform (And How This Checklist Fixes It)</h2>

<p>Here is a number that should stop you in your tracks: 88% of online consumers are less likely to return to a site after a bad experience, according to a study by Gomez and Akamai. For contractors, that stat is even more punishing. You do not get repeat visits from homeowners the way an e-commerce store might. You get one shot to make a first impression, and if your site fails on launch day, that homeowner calls your competitor instead.</p>

<p>The problem is not that contractors do not care about their websites. The problem is that launching a site involves dozens of moving parts, spread across technical infrastructure, content quality, search engine optimization, and user experience. Most contractors (and even many <a href="/services/website-design">web design</a>ers) rely on memory or informal processes. They forget to test the contact form. They skip mobile responsiveness checks. They launch with placeholder text still on the homepage. These are not small oversights. They are revenue-destroying mistakes.</p>

<h3>The Real Cost of a Botched Website Launch</h3>

<p>A plumbing contractor in Dallas we worked with last year spent $8,000 on a new site. The design looked great. The copy was solid. But on launch day, the phone number in the header linked to an old disconnected line. For three weeks, no one noticed. By the time they caught it, they had lost an estimated 47 qualified leads. At their average job value of $1,200, that is $56,400 in lost revenue from one broken phone link.</p>

<p>This is not an isolated story. We see versions of it constantly. An HVAC company in Phoenix launched a site where the "Request a Quote" button submitted to a dead email address. A roofing contractor in Atlanta forgot to set up SSL, so Google flagged the site as "Not Secure" and traffic dropped 60% in the first month. A landscaping business in Denver had beautiful portfolio images that took 8 seconds to load on mobile, and their bounce rate hit 78%.</p>

<p>Every one of these failures was preventable. Every one would have been caught by a rigorous website pre-launch checklist. That is what this article gives you. Not a vague list of "best practices." A step-by-step, contractor-specific website go-live checklist that you can work through systematically.</p>

<h3>What Makes This Checklist Different</h3>

<p>Most website launch checklists you will find online are written for generic businesses or SaaS companies. They tell you to "check your analytics" and "test your forms" without explaining what "test" actually means. They do not account for the specific challenges contractors face: <a href="/services/local-seo">local SEO</a> dominance, emergency service calls, service area pages, before-and-after galleries, license and insurance verification, and the intense competition in home services markets.</p>

<p>This checklist is built from 200+ contractor website launches. It is designed for roofers, plumbers, electricians, HVAC technicians, landscapers, painters, remodelers, and every other trade that serves homeowners. It prioritizes the items that actually move the needle for contractor <a href="/services/lead-generation">lead generation</a>. It skips the generic advice and focuses on what makes a contractor website convert visitors into booked appointments.</p>

<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> A contractor website launch checklist is a systematic validation process covering technical functionality, content accuracy, SEO setup, and launch day execution. It prevents broken features, missed leads, and search engine penalties that cost contractors tens of thousands in lost revenue.</p>

