<h2>Technical Checklist: The Foundation That Everything Else Builds On</h2>

<p>If your website's technical foundation is cracked, no amount of great copy or beautiful design will save it. This section covers the technical validation steps that must happen before any contractor site goes live. Skip these, and you are building on sand.</p>

<h3>Domain and Hosting Verification</h3>

<p>Start with the basics that are shockingly easy to mess up. Verify that your domain is properly registered and not expiring within the next 12 months. We have seen contractors launch a site on a domain that expires in 30 days, then forget to renew it. Set auto-renewal on. Confirm the domain registrar account is accessible and the contact email is current.</p>

<p>Check your hosting setup. For contractor sites, we recommend hosting with an uptime guarantee of 99.9% or higher. Shared hosting is fine for brochure sites, but if you are running appointment scheduling, live chat, or portfolio galleries with hundreds of images, consider managed WordPress hosting or a VPS. Test your hosting's server response time using tools like GTmetrix or Pingdom. Anything over 800ms is a problem.</p>

<p>Verify your SSL certificate is installed and working correctly. Every page should load over HTTPS, not HTTP. Check for mixed content warnings (when an HTTPS page loads HTTP resources). Browsers flag these aggressively now, and they kill trust instantly. A homeowner seeing "Not Secure" next to your URL will back out before they even see your hero section.</p>

<h3>Cross-Browser and Cross-Device Testing</h3>

<p>Your new website checklist must include real device testing, not just resizing your browser window. Test on actual iPhones, Android devices, iPads, and desktop computers. Check Safari, Chrome, Firefox, and Edge. Each browser renders CSS differently, and contractor sites often use complex layouts for service grids, testimonial carousels, and before/after sliders that break in unexpected ways.</p>

<p>Pay special attention to your navigation menu on mobile. The "hamburger" menu should open smoothly, links should be large enough to tap without zooming, and dropdowns should not require precision finger-work. We recommend a minimum tap target size of 48x48 pixels for all interactive elements.</p>

<p>Test your forms on mobile specifically. Typing into form fields on a phone is already annoying. If your fields are too small, if the keyboard covers the submit button, or if the form does not scroll properly, your mobile conversion rate will crater. According to Google, 61% of users will not return to a mobile site they had trouble accessing.</p>

<h3>Form and Conversion Path Testing</h3>

<p>This is where most contractor website launches fail. Every form on your site needs rigorous testing. That means:</p>

<ol>
<li>Fill out every form completely and submit it. Confirm the submission reaches the correct email address or CRM.</li>
<li>Test form validation. Submit with empty required fields, invalid email formats, and phone numbers with too few digits. Error messages should be clear and helpful.</li>
<li>Test the "thank you" page or confirmation message. Users need immediate feedback that their submission worked.</li>
<li>Test file upload fields if you have them (common for "send us photos of your project" forms).</li>
<li>Test autoresponder emails. If someone fills out your contact form at 2 AM, do they get an immediate "we received your message" email?</li>
</ol>

<p>Test every conversion path, not just contact forms. Click your phone number on mobile. Does it trigger a call? Click your email address. Does it open the user's email client with a pre-filled subject line? Test your "Get a Quote" buttons, your "Schedule Now" links, and any third-party booking integrations.</p>

<h3>Site Speed and Performance</h3>

<p>Page speed is a ranking factor for Google, and it is a conversion factor for every visitor. According to Portent, a site that loads in 1 second has a 3x higher conversion rate than a site that loads in 5 seconds. For contractors, where visitors are often in emergency mode ("my AC just died and it is 95 degrees"), speed is even more critical.</p>

