<h2>SEO Checklist: Setting Up Your Site to Rank Before It Goes Live</h2>

<p>SEO is not something you bolt on after launch. The foundation must be built correctly from day one. Getting it wrong means months of lost rankings while Google figures out (or fails to figure out) what your site is about. For contractors competing in local markets, early SEO mistakes can cede valuable positions to competitors.</p>

<h3>Keyword Research and On-Page Optimization</h3>

<p>Every page should target a specific primary keyword. Your homepage targets your broadest term ("roofing contractor Dallas"). Service pages target specific services ("emergency roof repair Dallas"). Blog posts target informational queries ("how long does a roof replacement take").</p>

<p>Verify that each page includes the primary keyword in: the title tag (under 60 characters), the meta description (under 160 characters), the H1 heading, the first paragraph, at least one H2 subheading, the URL slug, and image alt text. Do not stuff keywords unnaturally. Google's natural language processing is sophisticated enough to understand context and synonyms.</p>

<p>Check for duplicate title tags and meta descriptions. Every page should have unique metadata. Use a tool like Screaming Frog (free for up to 500 URLs) to crawl your site and identify duplicates, missing tags, and tags that are too long.</p>

<h3>Local SEO Setup</h3>

<p>For contractors, local SEO is everything. Homeowners search for services near them. If you are not optimized for local search, you are invisible to your best prospects.</p>

<p>First, verify your <a href="/services/gbp-optimization">Google Business Profile</a> is claimed, verified, and optimized. It should match your website's name, address, and phone number exactly. Any discrepancy ("St." vs "Street," different phone numbers, suite numbers formatted differently) confuses Google and hurts rankings.</p>

<p>Implement LocalBusiness <a href="/services/schema-markup">schema markup</a> on your site. This structured data tells Google your business name, address, phone number, hours, service area, and services offered. Use JSON-LD format and validate it with Google's Rich Results Test tool.</p>

<p>Build location pages for each city you serve. Each page should have unique content about that specific area. Mention local landmarks, neighborhood names, and area-specific concerns ("homes in this area often face hail damage from spring storms"). Do not create thin pages that just swap out the city name. Google penalizes doorway pages.</p>

<h3>Technical SEO Foundations</h3>

<p>Submit your XML sitemap to Google Search Console before launch. If your site is replacing an old site, set up 301 redirects from every old URL to the most relevant new URL. Failing to redirect old pages destroys your existing rankings and creates broken backlinks.</p>

<p>Check your robots.txt file. Make sure it is not blocking important pages. Make sure your staging site is either password-protected or blocked from indexing. We have seen contractors accidentally launch with their staging site indexed, creating duplicate content issues that took months to resolve.</p>

<p>Verify your site has a proper canonical tag on every page. This prevents duplicate content issues when the same content is accessible via multiple URLs (with and without "www," with and without trailing slashes, with URL parameters).</p>

<h3>Internal Linking Structure</h3>

<p>Internal links help Google discover your pages and understand your site structure. They also distribute "link equity" (ranking power) throughout your site. Check that:</p>

<ul>
<li>Every page is reachable within 3 clicks from the homepage</li>
<li>Your most important pages (top services) have the most internal links pointing to them</li>
<li>Navigation menus are logical and consistent across all pages</li>
<li>Footer links include key service pages and location pages</li>
<li>Breadcrumb navigation is implemented for deep pages</li>
</ul>

<p>Use descriptive anchor text for internal links. "Learn about our emergency plumbing services" is better than "Click here." Descriptive anchor text helps both users and search engines understand what the linked page is about.</p>

<h3>Image Optimization for Search</h3>

<p>Contractor websites are image-heavy. Portfolio photos, team photos, equipment photos, before-and-after comparisons. Every image is an SEO opportunity.</p>

<p>Rename image files before uploading. "emergency-plumber-fixing-burst-pipe-dallas.jpg" is better than "IMG_2345.jpg." Write descriptive alt text for every image. Alt text serves two purposes: it makes your site accessible to visually impaired users, and it gives Google context about the image content.</p>

<p>Compress images without visible quality loss. Use responsive images that serve different sizes for different devices. Implement lazy loading so images below the fold do not slow down initial page load. Consider using a CDN for image delivery if you have a large portfolio.</p>

<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> SEO setup before launch includes unique title tags and meta descriptions for every page, claimed and optimized Google Business Profile, LocalBusiness schema markup, 301 redirects from old URLs, XML sitemap submission, logical internal linking within 3 clicks from homepage, and fully optimized images with descriptive filenames and alt text.</p>

<h2>Launch Day Checklist: Executing the Go-Live Without Drama</h2>

<p>Launch day is not the time for improvisation. Every step should be planned, every contingency considered. This section covers the exact sequence we follow when taking a contractor website live.</p>

<h3>Pre-Launch Final Review (T-24 Hours)</h3>

<p>Twenty-four hours before launch, run a complete site crawl using Screaming Frog or Sitebulb. Export a list of all URLs, status codes, title tags, meta descriptions, and H1s. Review this spreadsheet for errors. Look for 404s, redirect chains, duplicate titles, missing meta descriptions, and pages with no H1.</p>

<p>Test every form one final time. Send test submissions from different devices and browsers. Confirm emails are received. Confirm CRM integrations are working. Confirm autoresponders are sending.</p>

<p>Review every page for spelling and grammar errors. Read copy aloud. Errors that your eyes skip over silently become obvious when spoken. Consider using a tool like Grammarly, but do not rely on it exclusively. It misses context-specific errors.</p>

<h3>The Launch Sequence</h3>

<p>When it is time to go live, follow this exact order:</p>

<ol>
<li>Put up a maintenance page if your site is currently receiving traffic</li>
<li>Point the domain to the new hosting/server (update DNS records)</li>
<li>Wait for DNS propagation (can take minutes to 48 hours, usually under 2 hours)</li>
<li>Remove any noindex tags or robots.txt blocks that prevented indexing during development</li>
<li>Verify the SSL certificate is working on the live domain</li>
<li>Test the homepage loads correctly</li>
<li>Test 5-10 key internal pages</li>
<li>Test all forms on the live domain</li>
<li>Submit the XML sitemap to Google Search Console</li>
<li>Submit the site for indexing via Search Console's URL Inspection tool</li>
</ol>

<p>Do not skip the noindex removal step. We have seen multiple contractors launch sites that remain invisible to Google for weeks because the development team forgot to remove noindex tags. Check your source code for <code>&lt;meta name="robots" content="noindex"&gt;</code> on every page.</p>

<h3>Post-Launch Validation (First 48 Hours)</h3>

<p>The first 48 hours after launch are critical. Monitor Google Search Console daily for crawl errors, indexing issues, and mobile usability problems. Check Google Analytics (or your preferred analytics platform) to confirm tracking is working and data is flowing.</p>

<p>Test your site speed on the live server. Hosting environments often perform differently than staging environments. A site that loaded in 2 seconds on staging might take 5 seconds on the production server if the hosting plan is underpowered.</p>

<p>Check for broken links using a tool like Broken Link Checker. New sites often have placeholder links, internal links pointing to staging URLs, or external links that were copied from templates. Fix every 404 immediately.</p>

<p>Monitor contact form submissions closely. If submission volume drops unexpectedly, something broke. If you receive a flood of spam, your form protection (CAPTCHA, honeypot) is not working.</p>

<h3>Common Launch Day Mistakes to Avoid</h3>

<p>After 200+ launches, we have seen the same mistakes repeatedly. Here is what to watch for:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Forgetting to update email addresses:</strong> Contact forms still sending to the web developer's test email</li>
<li><strong>Broken phone links:</strong> Click-to-call links with typos in the tel: protocol</li>
<li><strong>Staging URLs in content:</strong> Links or images still pointing to staging.yoursite.com</li>
<li><strong>Missing analytics:</strong> Google Analytics or Google Tag Manager code not installed on the live site</li>
<li><strong>Wrong business hours:</strong> Copy-pasted from a template and not updated</li>
<li><strong>Outdated copyright dates:</strong> Still showing last year (or worse, a template's placeholder year)</li>
<li><strong>Social media links pointing to templates:</strong> Facebook links going to facebook.com/template-page</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Launch day execution involves a final 24-hour crawl and review, sequential DNS propagation and noindex removal, immediate sitemap submission to Google Search Console, and intensive 48-hour monitoring for crawl errors, broken links, form failures, and analytics tracking issues.</p>

<h2>FAQ: Real Questions Contractors Ask About Website Launches</h2>

<p>These are the questions we hear most often from contractors preparing to launch new websites. The answers are based on real launches, real problems, and real solutions.</p>

<h3>How long does a contractor website launch typically take?</h3>

<p>A well-prepared launch takes 2-4 hours of active work, plus 24-48 hours of monitoring. DNS propagation is usually the longest variable. If you are switching hosting providers, plan for up to 48 hours for full global propagation. The actual site deployment (uploading files, updating databases, configuring settings) takes 1-2 hours for most contractor sites. Rushing the process increases error risk significantly.</p>

<h3>Should I launch on a specific day of the week?</h3>

<p>Launch on Tuesday, Wednesday, or Thursday during business hours. Avoid Monday (you are catching up from the weekend), Friday (you have less support availability if issues arise), and weekends (your web developer may not be available). Launching mid-week gives you two full business days to identify and fix issues before the weekend.</p>

<h3>What is the most commonly missed item on a website pre-launch checklist?</h3>

<p>Form testing. We estimate 30-40% of contractor websites launch with at least one broken form, incorrect email routing, or missing autoresponder. The second most missed item is removing noindex tags from staging. The third is updating contact information copied from templates.</p>

<h3>How do I know if my new website is ready to launch?</h3>

<p>Your site is ready when: every page loads without errors on desktop and mobile, every form submits correctly to the right destination, every link works, every image displays, page speed scores are 80+ on mobile, all content is final with no placeholders, SEO metadata is complete and unique, and you have tested the entire site on at least one real phone and one real computer.</p>

<h3>Do I need to tell Google when I launch a new website?</h3>

<p>Yes. Submit your XML sitemap through Google Search Console immediately after launch. Use the URL Inspection tool to request indexing for your homepage and key pages. If you are replacing an existing site, submit a change of address request in Search Console. If you have a new domain, expect 2-8 weeks for Google to fully crawl and index your site.</p>

<h3>What should I do with my old website when launching a new one?</h3>

<p>Set up 301 redirects from every old URL to the most relevant new URL. Do not redirect everything to the homepage. A page about "emergency plumbing" on the old site should redirect to the emergency plumbing page on the new site. Keep the old hosting active for 30-60 days to ensure all redirects are working. After that period, you can cancel the old hosting.</p>

<h3>How soon after launch will my contractor website start ranking?</h3>

<p>New domains typically take 3-6 months to rank for competitive local keywords. Established domains with proper redirects may see rankings transfer within 2-4 weeks. If you are in a less competitive market or targeting long-tail keywords ("emergency tankless water heater repair Scottsdale"), you might see rankings within weeks. Consistent content publishing and link building accelerate the timeline.</p>

<h3>What happens if I find a mistake after launch?</h3>

<p>Fix it immediately. There is no "grace period" where visitors forgive errors. A broken contact form costs you leads from day one. A misspelled headline damages credibility instantly. The good news is that most post-launch fixes are quick. The key is having a monitoring process that catches issues fast. Check your site daily for the first two weeks, then weekly for the first month.</p>

<h3>Should I announce my website launch on social media?</h3>

<p>Yes, but wait 48-72 hours after launch. This gives you time to catch and fix any issues before driving traffic. Announce on Facebook, Instagram, LinkedIn, and any other platforms where your customers are active. Include a specific call-to-action: "Book a free estimate through our new site" rather than just "Check out our new website." Consider running a small launch promotion (10% off for online bookings) to drive initial traffic and conversions.</p>

<h3>How much should a contractor budget for ongoing <a href="/services/website-maintenance">website maintenance</a>?</h3>

<p>Budget $100-300 per month for basic maintenance (updates, backups, security monitoring). Budget $500-1,500 per month if you are investing in ongoing SEO, content creation, and <a href="/services/conversion-optimization">conversion optimization</a>. A website is not a "set it and forget it" asset. WordPress core updates, plugin updates, security patches, content refreshes, and performance monitoring are ongoing requirements. Sites that are not maintained become slow, insecure, and outdated within 12-18 months.</p>

<h3>Can I launch a website myself, or do I need a professional?</h3>

<p>Technically capable contractors can handle simple launches, especially if using a hosted platform like Wix or Squarespace. However, WordPress launches involve more complexity (hosting configuration, plugin management, database setup, security hardening). For contractors whose website is a primary lead source, professional launch management is worth the investment. One missed redirect or broken form costs more than professional launch support.</p>

<h3>What is the difference between soft launch and hard launch?</h3>

<p>A soft launch means the site is live but you are not actively promoting it yet. You are testing with a small audience, monitoring for issues, and making adjustments. A hard launch means you are actively driving traffic through advertising, email announcements, and social media. We recommend a 48-72 hour soft launch period for all contractor websites. This catches issues before they reach your full audience.</p>

<h3>How do I track whether my website launch was successful?</h3>

<p>Define success metrics before launch. Common metrics for contractor sites: contact form submissions (target: match or exceed old site within 30 days), phone calls from the website (use call tracking numbers), organic traffic growth (target: 10-20% increase month-over-month for new sites), page speed scores, and search ranking positions for target keywords. Set up Google Analytics goals and Google Search Console before launch so you have baseline data.</p>

<h3>Should I keep my old domain or switch to a new one?</h3>

<p>Keep your old domain unless you have a compelling reason to switch (rebranding, legal issues, extremely poor domain history). Established domains have built-up authority, backlinks, and recognition. Switching domains means starting your SEO from scratch. If you must switch, plan for a 6-12 month recovery period and invest heavily in redirect management and authority building.</p>

<h3>What security measures should be in place before launch?</h3>

<p>Minimum security for contractor sites: SSL certificate (HTTPS), strong passwords on all accounts, limited login attempts, regular automated backups stored off-site, security plugin or WAF, and software update plan. If you collect payments online, you need PCI compliance measures. If you store customer data, you need encryption and access controls. Security is not optional. A hacked contractor site damages reputation and can expose customer information.</p>

<h2>Conclusion: Launch With Confidence, Not Hope</h2>

<p>A website launch is not a single moment. It is a process that starts weeks before go-live day and continues for months after. The contractors who win online are not the ones with the flashiest designs or the biggest budgets. They are the ones who treat their website launch with the same rigor they bring to a job site.</p>

<p>You would not install a water heater without checking every connection. You would not finish a roof without inspecting every shingle. Apply that same standard to your website. Work through the technical checklist. Validate every piece of content. Set up your SEO foundation correctly. Execute your launch day sequence methodically. Monitor relentlessly in the weeks that follow.</p>

